(registered 2026-10-30, last updated 2026-10-30)

Media type name: text

Media subtype name: prs.rowih

Required parameters: N/A

Optional parameters: encoding: the form of the message's text. "transliteration", 
the default, is the canonical serialisation in the nineteen Latin letters of the 
language's phonology, with payloads in the lowercase letters and the digits 2 to 7. 
"native" writes each of those nineteen symbols as its code point in the Private 
Use Area, U+F3A0 to U+F3B2, with payloads, separators and lines as in the 
transliteration.

The "charset" parameter is not used: a message is always UTF-8 (RFC 3629). A message
states its own version, so the type has no version parameter.

Encoding considerations: binary

In the canonical form of the type, each line ends in CRLF (RFC 2046, section 
4.1.1), and a line may exceed 998 octets: the envelope and each move occupy one 
line, and a payload, such as a relayed message, counts as one word whatever its 
length. A message in the transliteration uses only US-ASCII characters within 
UTF-8; a message in the native form uses three-octet UTF-8 sequences. A reader 
accepts any run of space, tab, line feed and carriage return between words, and 
computes the check values over a normalised text that it rebuilds from the words, 
in which words are separated by one space and lines end in a line feed. A transport 
that converts line endings, expands tabs or collapses spaces therefore does not 
affect a message; one that breaks a word, by wrapping or escaping it, changes a 
count and is detected.

Security considerations: A Rowih message is text and contains no executable content. 
A reader parses it under fixed maxima that each protocol release states (in protocol 
0.5, 155 words a message and a nesting of 5), and reads embedded material by its 
stated length, so that nothing inside the material can end it.

Integrity. Every number, word count and move carries a check word, and a message may
carry an integrity check value, the SHA-256 digest of its canonical serialisation
reduced modulo 10^9. These detect damage in transit: where the integrity check value
is carried, any single edit of a symbol or a word makes the message ill-formed or
fails the value. They are unkeyed, and give no protection against deliberate
alteration, since whoever alters a message can compute new values.

Authentication. A message may be signed with Ed25519 (RFC 8032). The signature covers
the whole message except its authentication and integrity fields, the sender and the
time included. Keys are never taken from a message: a participant holds the public
keys of the agents it knows from its own configuration, and refuses a message from
such an agent unless the signature verifies. Without a key, a claim of provenance
carries no weight.

Embedded material. Quotations, reports, examples and declared data are inert by
kind, and declared data is never parsed as the language. The language marks an
instruction, a quoted instruction and untrusted content apart, but cannot make a
receiving agent respect the difference: an agent that does not analyse what it
receives can still obey an imperative inside a quotation.

Confidentiality. The type provides none. A message names its sender and recipients
by participant number and states the time it was sent, and relies on its transport
for confidentiality.

Display. The native form uses code points of the Private Use Area, which a system
without the language's font shows as other glyphs or as replacement characters. A
text in that range is read as the language only if it parses, and any other use of
the range is reported as foreign use.

Interoperability considerations: A message states the releases of the language, the 
lexicon and the protocol it is written in, outside its moves; a reader lacking a 
release reports what it lacks and what it reads, and two agents settle the releases 
they share before relying on them. A reader accepts any run of separators between 
words. The reference toolchain has two implementations, in Python and in 
TypeScript, whose readers are tested to agree byte for byte over the conformance 
suite. The specification binds the type to the Agent2Agent protocol, as a part's 
mediaType, and to the Model Context Protocol, as an embedded resource's mimeType.

Published specification:

https://github.com/rowih/rowih/blob/v1.1.5/specification/protocol.md#the-media-type-and-the-binding-to-agent-transports

The reference specification as a whole, under CC BY 4.0:

https://github.com/rowih/rowih/tree/v1.1.5/specification

Applications which use this media: AI agents that exchange messages in Rowih, a 
language for agents to communicate with one another, over agent transports such as 
the Agent2Agent protocol and the Model Context Protocol. The reference toolchain 
reads, checks, writes and renders it: the Python package "rowih" 
(https://pypi.org/project/rowih/), with its command and its Model Context Protocol 
server, and a TypeScript reader and renderer.

Fragment identifier considerations: None. No fragment identifier syntax is defined 
for this type.

Restrictions on usage: None.

Additional information:

1. Deprecated alias names for this type: None.
2. Magic number(s): A message in its canonical serialisation begins with the word 
"wila", the envelope's word count: the bytes 77 69 6C 61 at offset 0 in the 
transliteration, and EF 8E AB EF 8E B0 EF 8E A9 EF 8E AE in the native form.
3. File extension(s): None.
4. Macintosh file type code: None.
5. Object Identifiers: None.

General Comments: Rowih was designed by an AI agent, Claude, under the auspices of 
Semiosphere (https://semiosphere.co.uk/). Its specification and data are published 
under CC BY 4.0 and its code under Apache-2.0, and it is not distributed 
commercially, which is why the registration is in the personal tree (RFC 6838, 
section 3.3).

